al herd of about 86.2 million cattle and calves.\n\nSo yeah importation is the only option as of now. \n\n", "author": "2021Is1776", "created": 1787337598363, "profile_picture": "", "community": "TheDonald", "ad_tier": 2, "last_comment_author": "1st10RtheBill0Rights", "is_video": false, "video_link": "", "is_new_user": false, "domain": "", "is_crosspost": false, "preview": "", "removal_source": "", "link": "", "author_flair_class": "", "is_removed": false, "media": {"resized_resolutions": [], "blurhash": "", "full_resolution": []}, "title": "for the retards regarding beef", "last_comment_created": 1787343756743, "content": "<p>-A normal cow can have one calf per year. Cows have a gestation period of about 9 months, and farmers manage herds on a 12-month calving cycle\n-to maintain a strict schedule of producing one calf every 12 months, ranchers manage cows on a tight annual production  cycle: Gestation: 9 months (283 days)Recovery &amp; Re-breeding: ~80 days after giving birth, the cow must successfully breed again to stay on track for the following year</p>\n<p>-In the United States, roughly 33 million calves are produced each year, meaning a similar number of female cows are bred annually to maintain the national beef and dairy herds.</p>\n<p>27.6 million beef cows in the United States, according to the USDA National Agricultural Statistics Service cattle report. This inventory is down 1% from the previous year, continuing a multi-year decline driven by past droughts and high ranch operating costs.</p>\n<p>we need\nTo sustain the population's current food habits, the United States requires a total living national herd of about 86.2 million cattle and calves.</p>\n<p>So yeah importation is the only option as of now.</p>\n", "score_down": 0, "author_flair_text": "", "pro_tier": 0, "is_stickied": false, "is_nsfw": false, "is_deleted": false, "is_image": false, "comments": 34, "score_up": 40, "is_edited": false, "is_moderator": false, "awards": 0, "vote_state": 2, "l